<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucius Aurunculeius Cotta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/54-bce">54 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Aurunculeius Cotta was a Roman legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>. In the winter of 54 BCE he shared command with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-titurius-sabinus">Quintus Titurius Sabinus</a> of a legion and a half quartered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atuatuca">Atuatuca</a> among the Eburones. He opposed Sabinus's decision to leave the camp on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>'s advice, and was killed when the column was ambushed and destroyed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Almost all that is known of Cotta comes from Caesar's Commentarii de Bello Gallico. During the Belgic campaign Caesar gave him and Quintus Pedius command of the cavalry. While Caesar made his first invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Cotta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-titurius-sabinus">Quintus Titurius Sabinus</a> were left in command of the legions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> to deal with the Menapii and the Morini, and afterwards the two returned from laying waste the lands of the Menapii, who had fled into their densest forests.</p><p>In 54 BCE, back from his second expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Caesar found food scarce and spread his eight legions among many Gallic states for the winter. Sabinus and Cotta, with a legion recently raised beyond the Po and five more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, wintered in the country of the Eburones, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catuvolcus">Catuvolcus</a>. Urged on by the Treveri, the Eburones attacked a wood-gathering party and the fort. Ambiorix then offered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> safe passage to another legion's camp, warning that a large force of Germans had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the council of war Cotta argued that they should not move without Caesar's order: Germans could be held off from behind Roman fortifications, the camp had plenty of supplies and help was near, and an enemy's news and advice should not be trusted. Sabinus feared a long siege and famine, the officers asked for a unanimous decision, and Cotta at last gave way.</p><p>The column left at dawn, heavily laden, and was attacked from front and rear in a ravine. Caesar writes that Sabinus lost his head, while Cotta kept calm and did his duty as a commander and as a soldier; the troops formed a square and held their own until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a> had them worn down with javelins. Cotta was reportedly hit in the head by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> stone. </p><p>When Sabinus sought terms, the wounded Cotta refused to surrender. Sabinus was surrounded and cut down at the parley, and Cotta fell with most of his men in the charge that followed. A few survivors killed themselves in the fort, and others reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a>. Caesar learnt of the disaster from prisoners taken by the garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-tullius-cicero">Quintus Tullius Cicero</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucius Aurunculeius Cotta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 54 BCE Ambiorix lured the legion and a half under Sabinus and <b>Cotta</b> out of Atuatuca and destroyed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/eburones">Eburones</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-64" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lucius Aurunculeius Cotta"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-64">What was here in 64 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
